But compiling is never that easy and there are a *lot* of options in
that configure script.

Partly due to NetHack's legacy code behavior of #define-ing every
fucking small feature and partly due to the different needs a client
or a server installation has.


I checked again, for a minimal working compilation on OSX you need to
tell it the location if you don't want to install it as root, you have
to tell what user id and group id the installation should have.

Additionally adding wizmode for the local user, it would look like that:
./configure --prefix=$HOME/unnethack --with-owner=`id -un` --with-group=`id -gn` --enable-wizmode=`id -un` && make && make install

This is not an installation that could be used as a binary
distribution package but it is enough for a local player. A simple
'~/unnethack/bin/unnethack' works afterwards.


But OSX compilation is still way easier than compilation on Windows.
At least on OSX you can expect that a compiler is installed.

